# Derivatives and where they exist, partial derivatives, the gradient,
# Hessian, Jacobian, Laplacian and directional derivatives, Wronskians,
# limits, Taylor series, implicit differentiation and antiderivatives.
# Every symbolic answer is followed by a numeric check.

# derivatives of any order; the conditions they hold under
diff abs(x)
diff sqrt(x)
diff log(x)
diff x*abs(x) x 2
diff sin(x) x 3
diff x^3 2
diff floor(x) + x^2
diff max(x, x^2)
diff x^(1/3)
myabs(x: real) = x > 0 ? x : -x
diff myabs
g(x) = x^3 - x
g''
g'(2)
myabs'(0)
# the n-th derivative from a pattern, checked for n = 1..6
diff exp(2x) n
diff sin(3x) n
diff log(2x + 1) n
diff 1/(2x + 1) n
diff x^5 n
# partial and mixed derivatives; no variable: all of them
diff x^2*y x
diff x^2*y^3 x y
diff x^2*y
partial x*y^2 y
diff sqrt(x^2 + y^2) x
f(x, y) = x^2*y + y^3
fx
fxy
diff x y
# gradient, Hessian (second-derivative test), Laplacian, Jacobian
grad x^2*y + sin(y)
grad x^2 + y^2 at 1, 2
hessian x^2 - y^2 at 0, 0
hessian x^2 + y^2 at (0, 0)
hessian x^3 - 3x*y^2 at 0, 0
laplacian x^2 - y^2
laplacian x^2 + y^2 + z^2 at 1, 1, 1
jacobian x*cos(y), x*sin(y) at 2, 0
jacobian (x + y, x*y, x - y) wrt x, y
ddir x^2*y along 3, 4 at 1, 1
ddir x^2*y along 3, 4 at 1, 1 raw
# Wronskians and linear independence
wronskian sin(x), cos(x)
wronskian exp(x), exp(2x), exp(3x)
wronskian 1, x, x^2
wronskian x, 2x
wronskian x^2, x*abs(x)
wronskian sin(x)^2, cos(x)^2, 1
# limits: substitution, L'Hopital, rewrites, one-sided, at infinity
limit sin(x)/x x -> 0
lim x->0 (1 - cos(x))/x^2
lim x->0+ 1/x
limit 1/x x -> 0
lim x->inf (1 + 1/x)^x
lim x->inf (3x^2 + 1)/(2x^2 - x)
limit sin(1/x) x -> 0
limit x*sin(1/x) as x -> 0
lim x->0+ x^x
lim x->0+ x*log(x)
lim x->inf sqrt(x^2 + x) - x
lim x->0 abs(x)/x
lim x->0 floor(x)
lim x->inf exp(-x)*x^5
lim x->-inf exp(x)
lim x->pi/2- tan(x)
lim x->0 sqrt(x)
limit sin(x)/x at inf
lim(x -> 1) (x^2 - 1)/(x - 1)
lim x->inf x*sin(x)
limit 1/x x -> 0 from right
# Taylor series, checked against Taylor-jet arithmetic and the function
taylor sin(x) order 7
taylor exp(x) at 1 order 3
maclaurin 1/(1 - x) order 5
taylor log(x) at 1 order 4
taylor sqrt(x) at 0
taylor abs(x)
taylor cos(x)*y x order 4
# implicit differentiation, checked on points of the curve
implicit x^2 + y^2 = 1
implicit x^2 + y^2 = 1 order 2
implicit x^3 + y^3 = 6x*y
implicit exp(y) + y = x
implicit a^2 + b^2 = 4
# antiderivatives (indefinite, + C, checked by differentiating)
antiderivative 1/(1 + x^2)
antiderivative 3/(x^2 + 2x + 5)
antiderivative 1/sqrt(1 - x^2)
antiderivative 1/sqrt(x^2 + 1)
antiderivative x/(x^2 + 1)
antiderivative x*log(x)
antiderivative x^2*exp(x)
antiderivative a*x wrt a
antiderivative x from 0 to 1
range of x^2 - 2x

# the same in English
differentiate x^3 sin x
what is the second derivative of x^4?
third derivative of sin(2x)
the nth derivative of exp(3x)
differentiate x^5 twice
d/dx sin(x)^2
d²/dx² x^3
d^2/dx^2 exp(-x^2)
derivative of x^3 at x = 2
the second derivative of sin x at pi/2
partial derivate of x^2 y wrt y
partial derivative of x^2*y with respect to x
partial of x*y^2 with respect to y
the mixed partial derivative of x^2 y^3 with respect to x and y
∂f/∂y
∂²f/∂x∂y
∂/∂y x^2 y
gradient of x^2 + y^2 at (1, 2)
∇f
the hessian of x^2 - y^2 at the origin
laplacian of x^2 + y^2
jacobian of x*cos(y), x*sin(y)
directional derivative of x^2 y along (3, 4) at (1, 1)
wronskian of sin x, cos x
wronkian matrix of e^x, e^2x, e^3x
are sin x and cos x linearly independent?
is x and 2x linearly dependent?
limit of sin x / x as x approaches 0
the limit as x goes to infinity of (1 + 1/x)^x
what does 1/x tend to as x goes to infinity?
limit of 1/x as x approaches 0 from the left
right-hand limit of abs(x)/x at 0
lim x→0 sin(1/x)
taylor series of sin x
maclaurin series of exp(x) up to order 4
taylor expansion of log x about 1 to order 3
dy/dx for x^2 + y^2 = 1
differentiate x^2 + y^2 = 25 implicitly
antiderivative of 1/(1 + x^2)
anti-derivative of x e^x
primitive of cos(x)^2
indefinite integral of x log x
the antiderivative of x^2 from 0 to 1
range of x^2 - 2x for x in 0..3
image of sin x
what values does x^2 + 1 take?
the codomain of cos x
the set of values of x^3 - x on [0, 1]
